---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---

<Base
  title="Our story"
  description="The story of Burn and Churn Coffee, a fictional café built as a security portfolio project."
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <span class="eyebrow">Our story</span>
    <h1>Small batch. Big character.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Burn and Churn Coffee is a neighbourhood café that roasts in small batches and pulls every
      shot like it matters. It's also <strong>entirely fictional</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>The café</h2>
    <p>
      The idea is simple: a short menu of coffee done properly. Espresso pulled as a double shot,
      brewed coffee from beans roasted that week, milk drinks with real texture, cold brew steeped
      slowly, and a few signature drinks that change with the seasons. No food, no tea, no fuss.
    </p>
    <p>
      The name comes from the two halves of the job: the <em>burn</em> of the roaster and the{' '}
      <em>churn</em> of a busy morning counter.
    </p>

    <h2>The people</h2>
    <p>
      Twenty people run the shop in this story: an owner, a store manager, three shift leads and
      fifteen baristas, with a part-time IT technician behind the scenes. They're invented, and so
      are their accounts, schedules and access reviews.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Burn and Churn is a security portfolio project. The café gives a realistic reason to build the
      things a real small business needs: a public website, staff sign-in, a counter register,
      online ordering and the security program around them. Everything is built to that program's
      controls and tested, with payments in Stripe's test mode only.
    </p>
    <p>
      To see how the site is built and secured, read <a href="/security/">Security</a>. For what
      data the site handles (very little), read <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/menu/">
        See the menu
      </a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
